# fleet-roster.example — a roster for bench/fleet-run.
#
# Copy it next to where you work (./fleet-roster) or to
# ~/.config/pitools/fleet-roster, and put the real player names in it. This file
# is an EXAMPLE and is deliberately not a real fleet: it exists so `fleet-run
# --dry-run -r bench/fixtures/fleet-roster.example 'uptime'` runs the whole loop
# on a machine with no radio, no nmcli and no players in earshot.
#
# Columns, whitespace separated. `#` starts a comment, blank lines are ignored:
#
#     <name> [ssid] [target]
#
#   name    the player's HOSTNAME. This is the identity fleet-run asserts before
#           it runs anything, and it is the one column that must be right —
#           every player answers on 10.0.0.1, so the address proves nothing.
#           It is the `hostrename@<name>` line in the card's /boot/starter.txt.
#   ssid    defaults to name. Renaming a player renames its SSID, so they match
#           unless someone has diverged them on purpose.
#   target  defaults to root@10.0.0.1. Give it something else to reach a player
#           over ethernet instead of its own hotspot.

# --- the bench player, real (pi-tools#t-025 smoke-tests against this one) -----
player-000

# --- example fleet rows: name only, ssid and target defaulted -----------------
player-066
player-067
player-068

# --- an SSID that diverged from the hostname ---------------------------------
player-069        biennale-069

# --- a player reached over ethernet instead of its hotspot -------------------
oyiri-48          oyiri-48        root@192.168.1.48

# --- a deliberate MISS: this hotspot is not on air, and never will be. --------
# Keep a row like this in a bench roster: it is how you see, in seconds rather
# than in a stall, that an absent player costs the pass nothing. It is also the
# second half of the pi-tools#t-025 smoke test.
player-absent
